(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-food-allergies-intolerances-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Food Allergies & Intolerances (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture's definitions, what distinguishes a food allergy from a food intolerance?","choices":["A food allergy involves an immune response, while a food intolerance does not.","A food allergy is caused by bacteria, while a food intolerance is caused by viruses.","A food allergy occurs only in children, while a food intolerance occurs only in adults.","A food allergy causes weight loss, while a food intolerance causes weight gain."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines food allergy as 'an adverse reaction to food that involves an immune response' and food intolerance as 'an adverse reaction to food that does not involve an allergic reaction.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which type of food allergy reaction does the lecture describe as often presenting soon after ingestion, being more violent, and potentially causing anaphylaxis?","choices":["Non-IgE mediated (late onset)","IgE mediated (early onset)","Cross-reactive syndrome","Delayed hypersensitivity syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states IgE mediated (early onset) reactions 'often present soon after digestion,' are 'often more violent,' and can include anaphylaxis. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed under 'late onset' reactions to food rather than 'early onset' in the lecture?","choices":["Urticaria and hives","Wheezing and cough","Food-sensitive colitis","Angioedema swelling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — food-sensitive colitis is listed under late onset reactions, along with diarrhea, abdominal pain, allergic rhinitis, atopic eczema, and constipation. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which specific herbs and spices does the lecture name as examples of allergenic foods?","choices":["Cinnamon, nutmeg, and clove","Coriander, paprika, and mustard","Basil, oregano, and thyme","Turmeric, cumin, and ginger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists coriander, paprika, and mustard under 'herbs and spices' as examples of common allergenic foods. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In the lecture's list of allergic reaction symptoms by body system, which cardiovascular findings are both marked with an asterisk as signs of rapid, potentially fatal anaphylaxis?","choices":["Itching and hives appearing across the skin","Nausea and vomiting in the GI tract","Low blood pressure and rapid heart rate (cardiovascular)","Runny nose and wheezing in the airway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture asterisks both low blood pressure and rapid heart rate under the cardiovascular system as indicators of rapid, potentially fatal anaphylaxis. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which fruit is explicitly listed in the lecture among the most common allergenic fruits?","choices":["Watermelon","Peach","Strawberry","Mango"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists apples, peaches, plums, cherries, banana, and citrus among common allergenic fruits. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"According to the lecture's diagnostic approach, what should a physical examination look for in suspected food allergy?","choices":["Elevated liver enzymes found on standard laboratory blood testing","Signs of an allergic reaction such as rash, itching, or intestinal bloating","Abnormal heart rhythm on cardiac auscultation","Weight loss over the preceding several months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states the physical examination should 'look for signs of an allergic reaction (rash, itching, intestinal bloating, etc.).' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the 'food challenge' step of food allergy diagnosis?","choices":["All suspected foods are reintroduced together at once in large quantities.","The patient must fast completely for 48 hours before any retesting begins.","Small amounts of excluded foods are added back one at a time, as long as anaphylaxis is not a possible consequence.","A sample of the suspected allergen is placed under the skin and monitored for inflammation."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines the food challenge as adding 'back small amounts of excluded foods, one at a time, as long as anaphylaxis is not a possible consequence.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the lecture state a blood test determines in food allergy diagnosis?","choices":["The presence of antibodies in blood that bind to food antigens tested","The patient's total white blood cell count on a complete blood count","Blood glucose response after ingesting the suspected food","Liver function markers measured following allergen exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines the blood test as determining 'the presence of antibodies in blood that bind to food antigens tested.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Per the lecture, which foods should a patient start with when beginning an elimination diet for suspected food allergy?","choices":["High-protein foods such as red meat, shellfish, and eggs","Low allergy foods such as rice, vegetables, and fresh meats","Dairy-based foods used to establish a symptom baseline","A variety of common allergens to identify triggers quickly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states the elimination diet should 'start out eating low allergy foods (rice, vegetables, and fresh meats).' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as part of the 'history' component of food allergy diagnosis in the lecture?","choices":["Genetic testing results from a specialty laboratory panel","The patient's typical weekly exercise frequency","The quantity of food required to produce a reaction","The patient's documented blood type on file"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture's history component includes 'quantity of food required to produce reaction' among other details. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the lecture identify as the cause of heartburn?","choices":["Bacterial overgrowth in the small intestine","Stomach acid backing up into the esophagus","Delayed gastric emptying","Inflammation of the gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states heartburn 'results from stomach acid backing up into esophagus.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Besides acid secreted by the stomach and H. pylori infection, what other common cause of peptic ulcers does the lecture list?","choices":["Overuse of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)","Excessive intake of dietary fiber from whole grains","Chronic dehydration from insufficient fluid intake","Overuse of over-the-counter antihistamine medications"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists 'overuse of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)' as a common cause of ulcers. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which drug pair from the lecture's 'Medicine Cabinet' slide are both classified as H2 blockers rather than proton pump inhibitors?","choices":["Omeprazole and Lansoprazole","Rabeprazole and Esomeprazole","Nizatidine and Famotidine","Cimetidine and Omeprazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists Nizatidine (Axid) and Famotidine (Pepcid) as H2 blocker examples, along with Cimetidine and Ranitidine. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the mechanism of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)?","choices":["They neutralize acid already present in the stomach.","They inhibit the ability of gastric cells to secrete hydrogen ions.","They block histamine receptors on acid-producing cells.","They slow gastric motility in order to reduce reflux episodes."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states PPIs 'inhibit the ability of gastric cells to secrete hydrogen ions and thus reduce acid production.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which treatment for constipation does the lecture specifically note should be used 'with supervision of primary care provider'?","choices":["Increased fiber consumption","Regular exercise","Establishing a regular bowel routine","Laxatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states 'Laxatives, with supervision of primary care provider can lessen constipation.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"According to the lecture, what are hemorrhoids?","choices":["Diverticula that protrude through the intestinal wall","Pouches in the large intestine caused by low fiber intake","An erosion of the stomach lining","Swollen veins of the rectum and anus, also called piles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines hemorrhoids as 'swollen veins of rectum and anus; also called piles.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the lecture define 'diverticulosis'?","choices":["The condition of having many diverticula in the large intestine","A single pouch that forms in the small intestine","Chronic inflammation occurring within existing diverticula","The surgical removal of diverticula from the colon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture defines diverticulosis as 'the condition of having many diverticula in the large intestine.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as part of IBS treatment, in addition to dietary changes?","choices":["Long-term antibiotic therapy course","Increasing daily caffeine intake","Surgical removal of the entire colon","Reducing stress and treating depression"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists 'reduce stress, treat depression' among IBS treatments. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What effect does celiac disease have on the small intestine, according to the lecture?","choices":["It creates diverticula along the intestinal lining.","It causes ulceration within the duodenal and gastric lining.","It thickens the intestinal wall, slowing motility.","It flattens the villi, limiting nutrient absorption."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states celiac disease 'flattens villi' and 'limits absorption of nutrients.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of the U.S. population does the lecture state is affected by celiac disease?","choices":["1%","5%","10%","15%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states celiac disease 'affects approximately 1% of U.S. population.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What distinguishes nonceliac gluten sensitivity from celiac disease, according to the lecture?","choices":["Gluten sensitivity requires lifelong avoidance of dairy, while celiac disease does not.","Gluten sensitivity produces intestinal damage identical to celiac disease.","Gluten sensitivity causes symptoms after gluten ingestion but no intestinal damage.","Gluten sensitivity is unrelated to any dietary trigger."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states nonceliac gluten sensitivity involves experiencing 'symptoms after ingestion of gluten, but no intestinal damage,' unlike celiac disease, which flattens villi. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which symptom cluster is listed under nonceliac gluten sensitivity in the lecture?","choices":["Sudden anaphylaxis and shock","Diverticula pouch formation","Headache, muscle pain, and joint pain","Gallstone formation in the gallbladder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists 'headache, muscle pain, joint pain' among the symptoms of nonceliac gluten sensitivity, along with GI distress, fatigue, weakness, and sleep disorders. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"By what percentage does the lecture state food allergies in children rose between 1997 and 2011?","choices":["25%","75%","100%","50%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states 'from 1997 to 2011, food allergies in children rose by 50%.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"According to the lecture, approximately how many childhood emergency room visits per year are caused by food allergies?","choices":["9,200","920,000","42,000","92,000"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states food allergies cause '92,000 childhood ER visits and 84 child fatalities per year.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The lecture's 'hygiene hypothesis' proposes that rising food allergy rates may stem from what?","choices":["Immune systems not being vigorously challenged by antigens due to a 'germophobic' society","Excessive early exposure to environmental allergens in childhood","Overuse of probiotic supplements added to infant formula","Increased consumption of processed sugar in the modern diet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture describes the hygiene hypothesis as a 'germophobic' society using antibiotics, hand sanitizers, and antimicrobial products, so 'immune systems not vigorously challenged by antigens' may become sensitized to innocuous substances like food proteins. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The 2006 Food Allergen Labeling and Consumer Protection Act mandated that manufacturers clearly identify which allergens on food labels, per the lecture?","choices":["Milk, eggs, fish, shellfish, peanuts, tree nuts, wheat, and soy","Only peanuts, tree nuts, and shellfish specifically","Gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley","Sulfites, MSG, and artificial food coloring agents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists these eight allergens as the major food allergens the 2006 Act required manufacturers to identify on labels. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does the lecture state the 2019 AAP guidance concludes about early introduction of peanut protein?","choices":["It should be delayed until the child reaches 12 months of age.","It has essentially no measurable effect on peanut allergy risk.","Maternal avoidance of peanuts during pregnancy is recommended instead.","Early introduction between 4 and 6 months of age reduces the risk of peanut allergy."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture states 'there is evidence that early introduction (between 4 and 6 months of age) of peanut protein reduces the risk for peanut allergies.' — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as a toxic food contaminant capable of causing food intolerance-type reactions?","choices":["Lactase deficiency","Monosodium glutamate (MSG)","Sulfites","Clostridium botulinum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists Clostridium botulinum as a 'toxic contaminant' under food contaminants. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which of the following is a synthetic compound added to foods that can cause food intolerance, rather than a naturally occurring food constituent?","choices":["Naturally ripened tomatoes","Fresh tropical pineapples","Monosodium glutamate (MSG)","Aged fermented red wine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture lists MSG under 'synthetic compounds added to foods,' along with sulfites and food-coloring agents. — RECORDED Nutrition Allergies and Intolerances CAM Putnam (4).pptx, Slide 30"}]};
